{
  "concept": "Three principal rays from the tip of an object refract through a converging lens and converge at one point, which fixes the position, size, and orientation of the real image formed for a given object distance.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "horizontal optical axis",
    "biconvex lens, shaded pale blue, centered on the axis",
    "vertical dashed line through the lens center",
    "red upward object arrow on the left of the lens",
    "label h_o with double-headed height arrow at the far left",
    "red dot focal point F on the left (near) side of the lens",
    "red dot focal point F on the right (far) side of the lens",
    "bracket labeled f from lens center to the right focal point",
    "ray 1 (red): parallel to the axis from the object tip, refracting through the far focal point",
    "ray 2 (green): straight through the lens center, undeviated",
    "ray 3 (purple): through the near focal point, emerging parallel to the axis",
    "red downward image arrow below the axis on the right, labeled h_i with double-headed height arrow",
    "horizontal bracket labeled d_o beneath the axis, from object to lens center",
    "horizontal bracket labeled d_i beneath the axis, from lens center to image",
    "numeric labels 1, 2, 3 beside the three rays near the object"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "objectDistance",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Object distance d_o",
      "range": [12, 300, 1],
      "default": 160,
      "teaches": "As the object moves relative to the focal length, the lens equation re-balances: the image point slides along the axis, the image height grows or shrinks, and the image flips from real and inverted (object beyond F) to virtual and upright (object inside F)"
    },
    {
      "id": "selectRay",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Each principal ray obeys one simple rule — parallel-then-through-F, straight through the center, or through-F-then-parallel — so any two suffice to locate the image and the third confirms it"
    },
    {
      "id": "measurementOverlay",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Measurement overlay",
      "options": ["Ray construction", "Measurements"],
      "default": "Ray construction",
      "teaches": "The geometric drawing and the algebraic quantities f, d_o, d_i, h_o, h_i describe the same configuration — the measured values update with the rays and satisfy 1/f = 1/d_o + 1/d_i and m = -d_i/d_o"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The three principal rays",
      "narration": "Three rays leave the object tip: one parallel to the axis bends through the far focal point, one passes straight through the lens center, and one through the near focal point emerges parallel. Click each ray to see its rule highlighted.",
      "state": { "measurementOverlay": "Ray construction", "objectDistance": 160 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The rays agree on one image point",
      "narration": "All three rays cross at a single point below the axis — that crossing alone fixes the inverted real image of height h_i at distance d_i, with no calculation needed.",
      "state": { "measurementOverlay": "Ray construction", "objectDistance": 160 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Attach the measurements",
      "narration": "Switching on the overlay attaches f, d_o, d_i, h_o, and h_i to the drawing, connecting the geometric construction to the lens equation and magnification.",
      "state": { "measurementOverlay": "Measurements", "objectDistance": 160 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the object",
      "narration": "Drag the object toward the lens: the image recedes and grows; at d_o = f the rays emerge parallel and no image forms; inside f the rays diverge and only a virtual upright image appears where their backward extensions meet.",
      "state": { "measurementOverlay": "Measurements", "objectDistance": 60 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Because each principal ray follows one trivial rule, their common intersection locates the image purely by drawing — and as d_o changes, that intersection moves exactly as the thin-lens equation predicts.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Landscape layout with the optical axis horizontal through the middle and the pale-blue biconvex lens slightly right of center, with a vertical dashed centerline. Red object arrow on the left standing on the axis, taller than the axis-to-lens spacing suggests; focal points as red dots labeled F symmetric about the lens with a bracket labeled f on the right. Ray 1 red (horizontal then down through far F), ray 2 green (single straight diagonal through lens center), ray 3 purple (through near F then horizontal); rays converge below the axis to the right where a short red downward arrow marks the image with h_i beside it. Brackets for d_o and d_i hang below the axis. Ray selection thickens/glows the chosen ray and pulses its defining marker (far F, lens center, or near F). When d_o < f, draw the refracted rays diverging on the right with dashed backward extensions meeting at an upright virtual image on the object side. Measurement overlay shows the f, d_o, d_i, h_o, h_i brackets plus current numeric values."
}

